::ENG4_U03_L06_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.R.12] Text A: The town should repair sidewalks near the clinic because inspection records list seven uneven sections. Text B: No patient should have to navigate broken concrete on the way to medical care. Which comparison best evaluates the rhetoric?
{
~A. Text A uses emotional language, while Text B uses only statistics.#Incorrect
=B. Text A builds practical credibility, while Text B creates urgency through values-based diction.#Correct
~C. Both texts use the same rhetorical choice because they support repairs.#Incorrect
~D. Text B is ineffective because it lacks inspection records.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L06_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Passage: "The plan is not a giveaway; it is a bridge to stable employment." What does this wording do?
{
~C. It frames the plan as irresponsible.#Incorrect
~B. It creates a humorous tone.#Incorrect
=A. It contrasts a negative label with a positive purpose.#Correct
~D. It avoids taking a position.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L06_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.R.07] Passage: A report begins with repeated customer complaints before recommending clearer product instructions. Why does the author likely begin this way?
{
=C. To show that the recommendation responds to a documented problem.#Correct
~B. To entertain readers with unrelated examples.#Incorrect
~A. To weaken the recommendation.#Incorrect
~D. To avoid identifying a purpose.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L06_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Text A argues for expanded parking using traffic counts. Text B argues for expanded parking using frustration from local shoppers. Which evaluation is most precise?
{
~A. Text A is always stronger because numbers are always persuasive.#Incorrect
~B. Text B is always stronger because frustration is more interesting.#Incorrect
~C. Both texts are unrelated because they use different support.#Incorrect
=D. Text A may fit planners, while Text B may fit business owners concerned with customer experience.#Correct
}
::ENG4_U03_L06_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.R.12] Text A: "The schedule change would reduce overtime costs by 9 percent." Text B: "The schedule change would give exhausted workers one predictable evening at home." Which rhetorical choice does Text B use?
{
=B. Human-centered framing that emphasizes worker experience.#Correct
~A. Statistical evidence to prove financial savings.#Incorrect
~C. Technical jargon to sound formal.#Incorrect
~D. Chronological order to explain a process.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L06_Q06::[MLA.ENG4.R.12] Text A uses the phrase "documented maintenance backlog." Text B uses the phrase "years of neglect." What is the best comparison?
{
=A. Text A sounds procedural, while Text B assigns blame and creates urgency.#Correct
~B. Both phrases are neutral and detached.#Incorrect
~C. Text B avoids judgment.#Incorrect
~D. Text A uses emotional accusation.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L06_Q07::[MLA.ENG4.R.07] Passage: A nonprofit appeal opens with a single student's story before requesting donations for scholarships. What purpose does this opening serve?
{
=D. To make the need concrete and emotionally accessible.#Correct
~B. To prove that every student has the same experience.#Incorrect
~C. To avoid making a request.#Incorrect
~A. To replace all evidence with entertainment.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L06_Q08::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Text A supports a wellness room with nurse visit data. Text B supports it with the claim that students need a calm place before stress becomes crisis. Which answer best compares the arguments?
{
=C. Text A uses institutional evidence, while Text B uses preventative and emotional framing.#Correct
~B. Text B opposes the wellness room.#Incorrect
~A. Text A is irrelevant because it includes data.#Incorrect
~D. Both arguments use identical evidence.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L06_Q09::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Passage: "This is not a minor inconvenience; it is a barrier to participation." What effect does this diction create?
{
~A. It minimizes the problem.#Incorrect
=B. It intensifies the problem by reframing it as an access issue.#Correct
~C. It makes the tone playful.#Incorrect
~D. It removes the author's position.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L06_Q10::[MLA.ENG4.R.12] Text A is a budget memo using cost estimates. Text B is a public speech using direct address and repeated "we." Which comparison is best?
{
=A. Text A fits an audience making financial decisions, while Text B builds collective responsibility.#Correct
~B. Text B is weaker because repetition can never persuade.#Incorrect
~C. Text A has no rhetoric because it uses numbers.#Incorrect
~D. Both texts have the same audience.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L06_Q11::[MLA.ENG4.R.07] Passage: A technology policy uses cautious words such as "limited rollout," "evaluation period," and "review point." What purpose likely explains this diction?
{
~A. To make the policy seem reckless.#Incorrect
=D. To reassure readers that the change will be controlled and assessed.#Correct
~C. To criticize the policy.#Incorrect
~B. To create comedy.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L06_Q12::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Text A supports later clinic hours with appointment data. Text B supports later clinic hours by describing workers who cannot leave day shifts. What is the best evaluation?
{
=C. Text A shows measurable demand, while Text B explains the human reason behind that demand.#Correct
~B. Text B is unrelated because it does not include appointment data.#Incorrect
~A. Text A is emotional because it includes appointments.#Incorrect
~D. Neither text makes an argument.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L06_Q13::[MLA.ENG4.R.12] Text A: "Survey results indicate that residents prefer online renewal." Text B: "Renewal should not require residents to stand in line for a task that takes two minutes online." Which rhetorical choice appears in Text B?
{
~A. Quantitative survey evidence.#Incorrect
~C. Detached technical language.#Incorrect
=B. Practical frustration and direct contrast between line waiting and online speed.#Correct
~D. Historical background.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L06_Q14::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Passage: A writer calls a proposal "a safeguard," while an opponent calls it "an obstacle." What does the word choice reveal?
{
=A. The writers frame the proposal differently, one as protection and one as interference.#Correct
~B. Both writers support the proposal equally.#Incorrect
~C. Neither word has connotation.#Incorrect
~D. The opponent uses neutral language.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L06_Q15::[MLA.ENG4.R.12] Text A uses a problem-solution structure with data. Text B begins with a vivid scenario and ends with a call to action. Which evaluation is best?
{
=D. Text A likely appeals to decision-makers, while Text B likely motivates public action.#Correct
~B. Text B cannot persuade because it begins with a scenario.#Incorrect
~C. Text A lacks rhetoric because it uses structure.#Incorrect
~A. Both texts are ineffective because they are organized differently.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L06_Q16::[MLA.ENG4.R.07] Passage: A principal's message uses inclusive language such as "our school" and "we can improve this together." What purpose does this likely serve?
{
=C. To build shared responsibility and reduce defensiveness.#Correct
~B. To blame one group publicly.#Incorrect
~A. To make the message purely informational.#Incorrect
~D. To hide the issue from readers.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L06_Q17::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Text A argues for park lights using incident reports. Text B argues for park lights by saying families should not avoid public spaces after sunset. Which audience would likely find Text B more effective?
{
=B. A community meeting focused on lived experience and public belonging.#Correct
~A. A technical engineering team requiring electrical specifications.#Incorrect
~C. A reader looking only for incident report numbers.#Incorrect
~D. A committee that forbids public comment.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L06_Q18::[MLA.ENG4.R.12] Text A says a plan is "cost-neutral after year two." Text B says the plan "pays back the community in safety and trust." Which comparison is most accurate?
{
=A. Text A uses fiscal framing, while Text B broadens value beyond money.#Correct
~B. Text B is purely technical.#Incorrect
~C. Text A is emotional.#Incorrect
~D. Both texts avoid persuasion.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L06_Q19::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Passage: "We are not postponing action; we are choosing a careful launch." What effect does this wording have?
{
=D. It reframes delay as responsible planning.#Correct
~B. It admits that action is impossible.#Incorrect
~C. It creates a sarcastic tone.#Incorrect
~A. It removes all author purpose.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L06_Q20::[MLA.ENG4.R.12] Text A appeals to expert testimony. Text B appeals to community identity. Which answer best evaluates their effectiveness?
{
=C. Expert testimony may establish credibility, while community identity may build personal commitment.#Correct
~B. Community identity can never persuade.#Incorrect
~A. Expert testimony is not rhetorical.#Incorrect
~D. Both appeals work only for the same audience.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L06_Q21::[MLA.ENG4.R.07] Passage: A letter to employees presents a policy change with a timeline, support contact, and transition period. What purpose most likely shapes these choices?
{
=B. To reduce uncertainty and help employees understand implementation.#Correct
~A. To entertain employees with workplace humor.#Incorrect
~C. To make the policy seem sudden and unsupported.#Incorrect
~D. To avoid explaining the change.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L06_Q22::[MLA.ENG4.R.08] Text A: "The program reduced absenteeism by 6 percent." Text B: "The program helped students feel seen before they disappeared from class." Which comparison best evaluates the information?
{
=A. Text A measures an outcome, while Text B interprets the human significance of that outcome.#Correct
~B. Text B contradicts Text A.#Incorrect
~C. Text A has no value because it uses a number.#Incorrect
~D. Text B has no rhetorical choice.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L06_Q23::[MLA.ENG4.R.12] Text A uses formal diction and passive construction in a policy memo. Text B uses direct address in a public message. Which evaluation is best?
{
=D. Text A may create institutional authority, while Text B may create reader involvement.#Correct
~B. Direct address always weakens writing.#Incorrect
~C. Formal diction has no effect.#Incorrect
~A. Passive construction always proves an argument is invalid.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L06_Q24::[MLA.ENG4.V.03] Passage: One author describes a rule as "consistent expectations." Another describes it as "one-size-fits-all control." What does the contrast show?
{
=C. One author frames the rule as fairness, while the other frames it as restriction.#Correct
~B. Both authors support the rule with identical tone.#Incorrect
~A. Neither author uses persuasive language.#Incorrect
~D. The second author uses neutral wording.#Incorrect
}
::ENG4_U03_L06_Q25::[MLA.ENG4.R.12] Text A is written for administrators and uses data, cost, and implementation steps. Text B is written for students and uses direct address, shared values, and urgent diction. Which final evaluation is strongest?
{
=B. Each text uses rhetorical choices suited to its audience, but Text A is stronger for policy approval because it gives decision-makers actionable evidence.#Correct
~A. Text B is stronger for every audience because urgent diction is always best.#Incorrect
~C. Text A is not rhetorical because it uses implementation steps.#Incorrect
~D. Both texts fail because they address different audiences.#Incorrect
}
